[Music]  two more sun care products are being  pulled from the shelves after johnson  and johnson warned that  benzene a chemical that may cause cancer  was found in some of its sunscreen  products  cvs health says it's halting the sale of  two store branded aloe vera items  out of an abundance of caution on  wednesday johnson johnson announced it's  voluntarily recalling  five new tredina and aveeno sunscreens j  j also said the move was out of an  abundance of caution  in may the lab valley shore reported  that it had found benzene in samples of  78 sun care products it tested including  some cvs health and j j  products we're joined now by dr  elizabeth hale a dermatologist  good morning doctor explain good morning  explain to us what is benzene exactly  and why is it so harmful  so benzene is a known chemical  contaminant it's actually used in the  manufacturing process of petroleum  you can find it in plastics and other  products it's something that's  ubiquitous in the environment and we're  exposed to it every day  by all mechanisms through our skin by  breathing it in by just walking around  cigarette smoke etc it is not a  sunscreen ingredient it's  it's important people understand it's a  contaminant and it's something that  somehow made its way in very small  amounts into certain sunscreens as part  of the manufacturing process  but we know benzene can be linked with  certain cancers  like leukemia and lymphoma it can  decrease blood counts  so it's definitely a problem but it is  not a sunscreen ingredient  so how concerned should people be we've  all been using sunscreen this summer  lots of people use it every single day  should there be a concern  and is there an amount that people you  know should be worried about if they've  used  so these are very minut amounts that  were detected in certain sunscreen  ingredients  and i think it was the responsible and  important thing to do to recall these  products  i got so many calls yesterday in the  office with people asking me just these  same questions  but i reassure them all the products  that we were recalled are  aerosols so for right now i'm telling  people maybe stick with a lotion  but i really want to convey how  important it is that people do use  sunscreen  you're not telling people here stop  using sunscreen apps in any way right  sunscreen is so important as part of a  sun protection strategy because skin  cancer  as you know is the most common cancer in  the united states and it's also the most  preventable  so skin cancer is linked with  unprotected sun exposure  even more than lung cancer is with  smoking so we really don't want people  to be so afraid that they stop  protecting themselves  they really still want to use sunscreen  just be aware of these products that  were recalled  and for the time being i wouldn't use  those products because i'm thinking liz  if it's my newt why tell us about it  because it makes me think they're  concerned about something right that's a  great question i think because  for a hundred years we've known benzene  as a problem not in sunscreens but  overall in the environment and it is a  known carcinogen so  i think what the companies all have to  do not just johnson and johnson but they  all need to do testing to see that their  products and confirm that they're free  of benzene  and hopefully you know we'll nip this  problem in the bud going forward we  mentioned there were 78  products it was found in but only only a  few have been recalled so far do you  expect this to go wider  there probably will be a few more  recalls if i had to guess just because  now everybody is really  scrutinizing and studying as they should  be um and checking within manufacturing  because again this is a manufacturing  issue not a sunscreen issue  so but right now all the products that  have been recalled sunscreen-wise are  aerosols and  everybody needs sunscreen especially  even people yes  yes people of color think they don't  need sunscreen right so skin cancer is  the most common and we're seeing a rise  all across the world and in all skin  types so everybody should protect their  skin all right dr elizabeth hale thank  you

